#######
Challenge description: Table: Logs

+-------------+---------+
| Column Name | Type    |
+-------------+---------+
| id          | int     |
| num         | varchar |
+-------------+---------+
In SQL, id is the primary key for this table.
id is an autoincrement column starting from 1.
 

Find all numbers that appear at least three times consecutively.

Return the result table in any order.


########
/*
First Solution: 
SELECT DISTINCT num AS ConsecutiveNums
FROM (
    SELECT num, num - LEAD(num) OVER(ORDER BY id) as lead_val, num - LAG(num) OVER(ORDER BY id) as lag_val
    FROM Logs
)
WHERE lead_val = 0 AND lag_val = 0;


*/##########
This code is inefficient however. Why? If we look at our table description, we notice that num is type varchar. 
This means SQL has to do typecasting to do the subtraction, not to mention also doing the subtraction which takes more runtime and compute power.
Look at my attempt 2 to see how I made this significantly more efficient.
